CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Congratulations on completing the first two weeks of this cours
 e!\n\nYou might be wondering “What could I contribute to the Circular Ec
 onomy?”\, or you might actually not know at this point why the Circular 
 Economy would benefit Africa. To support your learning and understanding o
 f the concept\, and to explore how it could benefit local communities you 
 get the opportunity to develop a CE solution with your group. \n\nThe fir
 st two sessions in this course were focused on giving you more insights on
  how CE organizes the use of materials and how businesses can organize the
 ir business model. You also learned what are the most common CE solutions 
 that entrepreneurs work with in Africa. With this knowledge we believe you
  and your group members are ready to embark on the journey of developing y
 our own CE solution. \n\nFormative assessment\n\nDescription:\nFor this a
 ssignment\, we want you to investigate your physical surroundings. Choose 
 an existing product that is currently being produced\, consumed\, and disp
 osed of according to the principles of the linear economy within a specifi
 c\, named community you have access to. You and your group need to propose
  a localized way to transition this product to a circular model.\n\nInstru
 ctions:\nCreate a recorded presentation (max 3 mins) to present your CE so
 lution to a potential local stakeholder or investor.\n\nWhat to include in
  your presentation\n\n* The Linear Reality (Evidence-Based): Describe the 
 product and provide original photographic evidence of how it is currently 
 sold\, used\, or disposed of linearly in your specific community.\n\n* Com
 munity Motivation & Primary Insight: Why this product for this specific co
 mmunity? You must include at least one insight or quote gathered from spea
 king directly with a local stakeholder (e.g.\, a shop vendor\, a consumer\
 , a local waste collector) about the current linear problem.\n\n* The Circ
 ular Transition: How will the product or service be redesigned?\n\n* Local
 ized Business Model: Provide an initial business model. Moving beyond gene
 ric ideas\, identify the specific local actors you would need to partner w
 ith (e.g.\, naming the specific local markets\, transport methods\, or com
 munity groups) to make this viable. What is the biggest local barrier you 
 anticipate facing?\n\nTips:\n\n* Use slides and visual aids. Be clear\, co
 ncise\, and engaging in your presentation. Use PowerPoint\, Canvas\, and L
 oom to create and record your presentation.\n\n* Follow a logical flow and
  introduce your business name\, the product and your motivation. \n\nAnne
 xure: \n\n* References: Include in-text references as well as a reference
  list of all researched information\, specifically including at least one 
 reference to a concept discussed in our Week 1 or Week 2 Live Sessions - P
 lease use APA 7 \n\n* Meeting Minutes: Minutes of your group meetings gen
 erated by an AI notetaker (including attendees).\n\n* Slide Deck: A copy o
 f your slide deck.
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Formative Assessment: Group Proposal [Circular_Economy_Fundamentals
 ]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://alueducation.instructure.com/calendar?include_context
 s=course_3067&month=09&year=2026#assignment_46047
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<header class="discussion-section clearfix">\n
 <div class="discussion-header-content right-of-avatar">\n<div class="discu
 ssion-header-right pull-right">\n<div class="new-and-total-badge"><span st
 yle="font-size: 18pt\; color: #169179\;"><strong style="font-family: inher
 it\;">Congratulations on completing the first two weeks of this course!</s
 trong></span></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</header>\n<div class="discussion-sec
 tion message_wrapper">\n<div class="message user_content enhanced" data-bi
 nd="message">\n<p>You might be wondering “What could I contribute to the
  Circular Economy?”\, or you might actually not know at this point why t
 he Circular Economy would benefit Africa. To support your learning and und
 erstanding of the concept\, and to explore how it could benefit local comm
 unities you get the opportunity to develop a CE solution with your group.&
 nbsp\;</p>\n<p>The first two sessions in this course were focused on givin
 g you more insights on how CE organizes the use of materials and how busin
 esses can organize their business model. You also learned what are the mos
 t common CE solutions that entrepreneurs work with in Africa. With this kn
 owledge we believe you and your group members are ready to embark on the j
 ourney of developing your own CE solution.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><span style="fon
 t-size: 14pt\; color: #169179\;"><strong>Formative assessment</strong></sp
 an></p>\n<p><strong>Description:</strong><br>For this assignment\, we want
  you to investigate your physical surroundings. Choose an existing product
  that is currently being produced\, consumed\, and disposed of according t
 o the principles of the linear economy <em>within a specific</em>\, <em>na
 med community you have access to</em>. You and your group need to propose 
 a localized way to transition this product to a circular model.</p>\n<p><s
 pan style="font-size: 14pt\;"><strong>Instructions:</strong></span><br>Cre
 ate a recorded presentation (max 3 mins) to present your CE solution to a 
 potential local stakeholder or investor.</p>\n<p><span>What to include in 
 your presentation</span></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The Linear Reality (Evidence-Based
 ): Describe the product and provide original photographic evidence of how 
 it is currently sold\, used\, or disposed of linearly in your specific com
 munity.</li>\n<li>Community Motivation &amp\; Primary Insight: Why this pr
 oduct for this specific community? You must include at least one insight o
 r quote gathered from speaking directly with a local stakeholder (e.g.\, a
  shop vendor\, a consumer\, a local waste collector) about the current lin
 ear problem.</li>\n<li>The Circular Transition: How will the product or se
 rvice be redesigned?</li>\n<li>Localized Business Model: Provide an initia
 l business model. Moving beyond generic ideas\, identify the specific loca
 l actors you would need to partner with (e.g.\, naming the specific local 
 markets\, transport methods\, or community groups) to make this viable. Wh
 at is the biggest local barrier you anticipate facing?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><sp
 an style="font-size: 14pt\;"><strong>Tips:</strong></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>
 Use slides and visual aids. Be clear\, concise\, and engaging in your pres
 entation. Use PowerPoint\, Canvas\, and Loom to create and record your pre
 sentation.</li>\n<li>Follow a logical flow and introduce your business nam
 e\, the product and your motivation.&nbsp\;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style="f
 ont-size: 14pt\;"><strong>Annexure:</strong></span>&nbsp\;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>
 <span style="color: #e62429\;"><strong>References: Include in-text referen
 ces as well as a reference list of all researched information\, specifical
 ly including at least one reference to a concept discussed in our Week 1 o
 r Week 2 Live Sessions - Please use APA 7&nbsp\;</strong></span></li>\n<li
 ><span style="color: #e62429\;"><strong>Meeting Minutes: Minutes of your g
 roup meetings generated by an AI notetaker (including attendees).</strong>
 </span></li>\n<li><span style="color: #e62429\;"><strong>Slide Deck: A cop
 y of your slide deck.</strong></span></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>
END:VEVENT
